# DemoKit Integration Rules

This project uses DemoKit (https://github.com/Kasava-AI/demokit) to create interactive product demos by intercepting API calls and returning mock data.

## Package Selection

Install `@demokit-ai/core` and `@demokit-ai/react`. This works for any React app, including TanStack Query and SWR apps (standard network interception, no adapter needed) and Next.js apps that fetch client-side.

## Fixture Patterns

Fixtures define the mock data returned when demo mode is active.

- Place fixtures in `lib/fixtures.ts` or `lib/demokit/fixtures.ts`
- Use the format `'METHOD /api/path/:param'` as fixture keys
- Prefer function fixtures over static objects for dynamic behavior
- Always destructure `params`, `body`, `searchParams` as needed

```typescript
// Good: function fixture with params
'GET /api/users/:id': ({ params }) => ({ id: params.id, name: 'Demo User' })

// Good: static fixture for simple responses
'GET /api/config': { theme: 'dark', features: ['billing', 'analytics'] }

// Good: POST with body
'POST /api/users': ({ body }) => ({ id: crypto.randomUUID(), ...body })
```

## Provider Setup

### React
```tsx
import { DemoKitProvider, DemoModeBanner } from '@demokit-ai/react'
<DemoKitProvider fixtures={fixtures}>
  <DemoModeBanner />
  <App />
</DemoKitProvider>
```

## Data Generation Guidelines

When generating fixture data:
- Create realistic, domain-appropriate data (not "lorem ipsum" or "test123")
- Include believable names, emails, dates, and prices
- Maintain referential integrity (order.user_id should reference an existing user)
- Generate at least 2 named scenarios: `default` (happy path) and `empty-state`
- Consider edge cases: empty arrays, null optional fields, items in various states
- Use consistent IDs across related entities (UUIDs preferred)

## Scenarios

Scenarios are named sets of fixtures for different demo states:

```typescript
export const scenarios = {
  default: defaultFixtures,
  'empty-state': emptyFixtures,
  'enterprise': enterpriseFixtures,
  'error-state': errorFixtures,
}
```

## Demo Mode Activation

- URL parameter: `?demo=true` on any page
- React hook: `useDemoMode()` returns `{ isDemoMode, toggle, enable, disable }`
- Programmatic: `demo.enable()` / `demo.disable()` / `demo.toggle()`

## Session State

DemoKit supports mutable state within a demo session (e.g., cart operations):

```typescript
'POST /api/cart': ({ body, session }) => {
  const cart = session.get('cart') ?? []
  cart.push(body.item)
  session.set('cart', cart)
  return { items: cart }
}
```

## Important Notes

- DemoKit intercepts `globalThis.fetch` at the client level - no backend changes needed
- Fixtures only activate when demo mode is enabled
- Session state persists across page refreshes (localStorage by default)
- All packages are published under the `@demokit-ai` scope on npm
- For AI-powered narrative data generation, see DemoKit Cloud (https://demokit.dev/cloud)
